### 4.5 What the results ask of related work

Several of these results say less about misinformation than about how misinformation data are built and used. Each points to a check that related work can adopt.

**Misinformation research.** A Fake/Real comparison describes a dataset as much as it describes misinformation. The same estimand gave ratios from about a quarter in CoAID to about two in GossipCop. PolitiFact, which shares CoAID's fact-check construct, moved from below 1 to above 1 when one cluster of items sharing tweets was set aside. Studies should therefore say what both classes consist of, because CoAID's Real category of health agencies and health-information publishers shaped its gap as much as its Fake label did. They should also say whether the outcome counts every item or only items that circulated. In CoAID the whole gap lay in whether items were posted at all, which a design that starts from cascades cannot see. Comparisons across datasets need a common estimand, such as standardized expected counts rather than coefficients from different model families. They also need uncertainty that respects shared posts, because a single dependent cluster changed the magnitude of CoAID's contrast and the direction of PolitiFact's.

**Machine-learning work.** A high within-dataset score can measure how a dataset was assembled rather than how detectable misinformation is. In CoAID, source and label were nearly the same variable (Cramér's V 0.971). Real texts carried years and photo credits that Fake texts almost never did, and encoding errors appeared only in Fake texts. Removing source names, encoding errors, or dates and credits barely changed CoAID's 0.926 macro F1. The signal is spread across many collection-linked cues, so no single cleanup removes it, and the same model transferred poorly and learned word associations unrelated to FakeHealth's. Five practical checks follow:

- audit how source and label co-vary before training;
- scan for formatting and encoding markers that differ by class;
- group data splits by resource, version lineage and near-duplicate text rather than by row;
- report transfer on the same target items alongside within-dataset scores;
- state the text representation, because titles, excerpts and full articles give a model different information.

**Data processing.** Many of the decisions that most affected these results were made before any model ran. In an early representation of CoAID, generic branding and domain-level links merged distinct pages into one item joining 110 source records. Engagement counts became meaningful only after items were resolved by specific resource and version lineage. Items that share posts are not independent rows; treating them as clusters set the width of every engagement interval here. Engagement scope also has to match across datasets. We excluded FakeHealth's retweets because CoAID has no equivalent, and pooled CoAID posts across its four releases, and both choices change the counts. Collection methods have to be compared, not assumed. Both health datasets searched for posts by title, so search alone was not the difference, but query terms, crawl dates and release pooling were not held equal. If dataset releases documented these steps, others could tell a finding about misinformation from a finding about its data.

